# Without a file here, what git does to a text file on the way into the
# repository and on the way back out is a fact of each clone's core.autocrlf
# rather than of this tree, so two clones of the same commit can hold different
# bytes. That is untidy for source and wrong for evidence.
#
# Source and configuration are text. They are stored with a line feed and checked
# out the way the platform prefers, which is what a Windows editor and a Linux
# build each expect.
* text=auto

# Subtitle fixtures are bytes rather than text, and this is the line the rest of
# the file exists for. SubRip ends every line with a carriage return and a line
# feed, and a fixture exists to prove the exact bytes the writer produced.
#
# The conversion that would destroy that happens on the way in, not on the way
# out. Under the rule above git stores a text file with the carriage returns
# removed, and it is the checkout that puts them back, on the platforms that ask
# for them and nowhere else. So the same commit gives a Windows clone a file with
# carriage returns and a Linux clone one without, the fixture no longer names one
# set of bytes, and the test comparing the writer against it passes on one
# platform and fails on the other for a reason that has nothing to do with the
# writer. Measured on this fixture: 170 bytes stored under the rule below, 158
# stored under the rule above.
*.srt -text

# Shell scripts are read by a program that treats a carriage return as part of
# the command. Under the rule above a Windows clone would check one out with
# them, and running it there fails on the first line with an error naming a
# command nobody wrote. These are line feed everywhere, on every platform.
*.sh text eol=lf
